{"id":3752,"date":"2021-09-24T09:29:33","date_gmt":"2021-09-24T16:29:33","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3752"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:35:50","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:35:50","slug":"4-3-temperature-and-air-pressure","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/4-3-temperature-and-air-pressure\/","title":{"rendered":"4.3: Temperature and Air Pressure"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51ac28\" name=\"6a2214b51ac28\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Describe what temperature is measured by the CoDrone Mini&#8217;s temperature sensor<\/li>\n<li>Explain the importance of the temperature measurement<\/li>\n<li>Create a program that can limit flight based on drone temperature<\/li>\n<li>Convert temperature measurements to different units<\/li>\n<li>Describe what a barometer is and how it can be used to measure height<\/li>\n<li>Use barometer measurements to predict the height of the drone<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Temperature Sensor<\/li>\n<li>Print the Current Temperature<\/li>\n<li>Temperature in Flight<\/li>\n<li>Celsius to Fahrenheit<\/li>\n<li>Barometer<\/li>\n<li>Print the Current Pressure<\/li>\n<li>More Pressure Readings<\/li>\n<li>Pressure Readings and Lists<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Height Calculator Part 1<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Height Calculator Part 2<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51ad30\" name=\"6a2214b51ad30\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Temperature Sensor<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">One of the sensors onboard CoDrone Mini is the temperature sensor, which does exactly what it says: it reads the temperature. However, it does NOT get the temperature from its environment, so it cannot be used to tell you whether it\u2019s warm or cold where you are. What it can tell you is its internal temperature, so how cold or hot it is inside of the drone. This number will be in degrees Celsius.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51ada2\" name=\"6a2214b51ada2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Print the Current Temperature<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Find out what your CoDrone Mini\u2019s starting temperature is! Import the CoDrone Mini library, create the drone object, pair, and then create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">temperature<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">that will store the drone\u2019s temperature after using the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_drone_temp()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">function. Next, include a print statement that will print <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">temperature<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">\u2019s value in degrees Celsius.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ntemperature = drone.get_drone_temp()\r\n\r\nprint(temperature)\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Run the program to see how hot it is inside of your drone. Are you surprised by the results?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51ae27\" name=\"6a2214b51ae27\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Temperature in Flight<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">One thing about batteries that you might already know: they heat up when they\u2019re being used. You took your CoDrone Mini\u2019s internal temperature while it was just sitting there, but what happens when it\u2019s in flight and the battery is working hard to keep your drone in the air?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Import the CoDrone Mini library, create the drone object, and pair. Next, create a temperature variable (we called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_temp<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">) and set it to get the drone\u2019s temperature. Include a line to print the temperature before takeoff.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Have your drone takeoff, hover, and land. Next, create another variable to get the drone\u2019s temperature, but name this one something different. we name ours\u00a0 <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">stop_temp<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">. Include a line to print the temperature after takeoff. Remember, naming your variables is important. We should always give them meaningful names. In this case, start and stop or begin and end temps would be great, descriptive options.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Finally, include code that will subtract the values from the two temperature variables and print the difference.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nstart_temp = drone.get_drone_temp()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Before taking off: \", start_temp)\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.hover(10)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\n\r\nstop_temp = drone.get_drone_temp()\r\n\r\nprint(\"After taking off: \" , stop_temp)\r\n\r\n\r\ndiff_temp = stop_temp - start_temp\r\n\r\nprint(\"Difference: \", diff_temp)\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51ae95\" name=\"6a2214b51ae95\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Celsius to Fahrenheit<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Fun fact: most of the world uses Celsius to measure temperature, and under 10% of countries use Fahrenheit. <\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/worldpopulationreview.com\/country-rankings\/countries-that-use-fahrenheit\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">If you\u2019re in one of the Fahrenheit countries<\/span><\/a><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">, or if you just want your CoDrone Mini to do some extra math, you can write a function to convert the Celsius input to degrees Fahrenheit.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">def to_fahrenheit(degrees_c):\r\n\r\n    degrees_f = degrees_c * 1.8 + 32\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 return degrees_f<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Try including this function in one of your programs! For example:<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndef to_fahrenheit(degrees_c):\r\n    degrees_f = degrees_c * 1.8 + 32\r\n    return degrees_f\r\n\r\ntemperature = drone.get_drone_temp()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Celsius: \", temperature)\r\nprint(\"Fahrenheit: \", to_fahrenheit(temperature))\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51aeee\" name=\"6a2214b51aeee\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Barometer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">In addition to the temperature sensor, the barometer can also measure pressure. You may have noticed that when you&#8217;re in the mountains or on a plane, your ears pop! The reason this happens is because there is air trapped in your inner ear. When you move up to a higher altitude, the air pressure is lower. This change causes pressure on your eardrum, which sounds and feels a lot like popping.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Our ears only pop with big differences in altitude. It probably wouldn&#8217;t happen if you went from the first floor of a building to the second floor. However, your CoDrone has a pressure sensor, also known as a barometer, built in to measure the air pressure around the CoDrone Mini. That sensor can detect seemingly tiny differences in height. Barometers are often used in weather forecasting since changes in air pressure can affect the weather. For example, a drop in atmospheric pressure means a storm is most likely on the way, while an increase in pressure usually predicts clear skies.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51afa5\" name=\"6a2214b51afa5\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Print the Current Pressure<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Find out what your CoDrone Mini\u2019s starting pressure is! Import the CoDrone Mini library, create the drone object, pair, and then create a variable called <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">pressure<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> that will store the drone\u2019s air pressure after using the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_pressure()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">function. Next, include a print statement that will print <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">pressure<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">\u2019s value in pascals, which is the international standard for air pressure measurement.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\npressure = drone.get_pressure()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Pressure: \", pressure)\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Try running your program with the CoDrone at different altitudes. What do you notice about the difference in air pressure?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51b029\" name=\"6a2214b51b029\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>More Pressure Readings<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Let\u2019s take what you learned and see it in action! Import the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, create the drone object, and pair with your CoDrone.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Create a pressure variable that will get and store the air pressure at ground level. Important: you will be creating multiple pressure variables, one for each height you want to measure the air pressure at. You should either make their names memorable (like a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">pressure_ground<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">) or use numbers (like \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">pressure_1<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">).<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Program your drone to take off and then create another pressure variable. This will get the air pressure at the takeoff altitude.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Have your drone throttle up at 50% speed for 1 second. Create another pressure variable that will get their air pressure at this level.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">You can keep repeating the last step until you have all of the altitudes you need or your drone hits the ceiling &#8212; whatever comes first!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Finally, have your drone land. You should then include print statements so you can see the air pressure at all of the levels you included.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\npressure_1 = drone.get_pressure() # ground level\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\npressure_2 = drone.get_pressure() # after takeoff level 1\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.set_throttle(50)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\npressure_3 = drone.get_pressure() # level 2\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\npressure_4 = drone.get_pressure() # level 3\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\n\r\nprint(\"On the Ground: \", pressure_1)\r\n\r\nprint(\"Level 1: \", pressure_2)\r\n\r\nprint(\"Level 2: \", pressure_3)\r\n\r\nprint(\"Level 3: \", pressure_4)\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51b0a9\" name=\"6a2214b51b0a9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Pressure Readings and Lists<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">You will be using your program from the last activity but modifying it to use a list instead of four completely separate variables!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">After importing libraries, creating the drone object, and pairing, create a list called <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">labels <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">and add the levels where you will be reading the drone\u2019s air pressure. If you want to measure your drone\u2019s air pressure at more or less levels, you will need to add or remove those from the list. Then create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">pressure_data<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">. This is what will store the air pressure\u2019s values.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Add a command that will get the drone\u2019s air pressure at ground level and then append, or add, that reading to your list. Since this is the first reading, this will go to the <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">\u201cGround\u201d<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> label.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Add this same command after the drone\u2019s takeoff, the throttle up after takeoff, and any other level where you decide to measure the air pressure. The readings will go to their respective labels in the list.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Add \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.sleep(0.05)<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">so the program doesn\u2019t skip over the landing command and then land your drone.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Finally, print the length of your list with both the labels and their pressure data. Try graphing the results to see if you notice a pattern in your data!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nlabels = [\"Ground\", \"Level 1\", \"Level 2\", \"Level 3\"]\r\n\r\npressure_data = []\r\n\r\n\r\npressure_data.append(drone.get_pressure()) # ground level\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\npressure_data.append(drone.get_pressure()) # after takeoff level 1\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.set_throttle(50)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\npressure_data.append(drone.get_pressure()) # level 2\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\npressure_data.append(drone.get_pressure()) # level 3\r\n\r\n\r\ntime.sleep(0.05) # to prevent skipping land command\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range (len(labels)):\r\n\r\n    print(labels[i], pressure_data[i])\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51b0fa\" name=\"6a2214b51b0fa\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: Height Calculator Part 1<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, we will first take, store and print barometer measurements. We will then attempt to correlate those to the height of the drone.<\/p>\n<p>Define a function that takes readings and appends them to a list of pressures every second. You must use a loop to accomplish this. The function should take in a duration or number of readings as a parameter. Have the drone take off, call the function for a time of your choosing. Have the drone throttle up at a throttle level of your choosing for the duration that you chose (it is recommended to use a small number for both). Finally,\u00a0 use a loop to print the values stored in the pressures. When you run your program, use something to measure the height of the drone at takeoff and the approximate height at each 1 second interval (you could use the number of blocks on the wall from the floor or a measuring stick or even your body).[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214b51b14b\" name=\"6a2214b51b14b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 2: Height Calculator Part 2<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that we have a list of pressures and heights, let&#8217;s figure out our conversion ratio. Complete the following calculation 3-4 sets of measurements you took:<\/p>\n<p>height \/ pressure<\/p>\n<p>This is your conversion ratio. Whenever you multiply this by a pressure, the Pascals will cancel and leave you with a height. Now that we know this ration, we will program it into the second part of our program:<\/p>\n<p>Take your part 1 challenge program and add a function that takes in a list of pressure values and converts them to heights and then prints the list. You will need two loops for this function. Add the function call at the end of the program and then change the value of the duration. Run the program and watch your drone. Now check the the height values that were printed. Do the values printed out correspond to how high your drone flew? Would changing the throttle change the ratio we created? Why or why not?<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 12 Goals and Steps to Success [vc_column_text]Goals: By the end of this lesson, I will be able to: Describe what temperature is measured by the CoDrone Mini's temperature&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/4-3-temperature-and-air-pressure\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4147,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3473,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1782,1848,1770,1793,1826,1438,1482,853,144,694,1392,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2158,2159,2125,2156,2122,2134,2155,2157],"class_list":["post-3752","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-air-pressure","lesson-tag-barometer","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-intermediate","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-sensor","lesson-tag-temperature","module-level-4","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3752","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":10,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3752\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":4507,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3752\/revisions\/4507"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4147"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=3752"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=3752"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=3752"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}